.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[CR]cbor::schema<T>\f[R] writes a struct as Packed CBOR and reads each
field of fixed size at an offset that the compiler computes.
It reads only the description of each struct and builds on every
compiler.
With reflection it also checks each description against the members.
\f[CR]path\f[R] opens a message in O(1) of its size, and a read of a
field of fixed size is one jump that checks only the bytes it reads.
\f[CR]at_path\f[R] with no accessor allocates nothing.
\f[CR]encode\f[R] is O(bytes), and a list of fixed\-size structs is one
copy.
.PP
The examples use the types in test/doc_types.hpp, namespace
\f[CR]doc_reference_schema\f[R].
.SS cbor::schema<T>::encode
Writes \f[CR]value\f[R] as one Packed CBOR message (\f[CR]wire\f[R]).
The first form returns a new \f[CR]std::string\f[R].
The second form appends to \f[CR]target\f[R] and returns the count of
bytes of the message.
\f[CR]target\f[R] is a \f[CR]std::string\f[R], a container of
\f[CR]char\f[R], a \f[CR]std::span<char>\f[R] or a message writer.
Only public members are written.
Each field has a head of fixed width.
So a value does not change the offset of a field.
An error of the message writer comes back as its \f[CR]cbor::error\f[R].
A struct with no cbor::tag, two structs with one tag, or a tag of a
standard type does not compile.
.SS cbor::schema<T>::decode
Reads the whole message into a \f[CR]T\f[R] and returns it in a
\f[CR]cbor::ref<T>\f[R] that holds the owner of the bytes.
A \f[CR]std::string_view\f[R] member views the bytes.
Nothing is copied: an rvalue moves into a new owner, and an owner is
read in place.
An lvalue \f[CR]std::string\f[R] does not compile.
\f[CR]target\f[R] gives the start value, for example \f[CR]std::pmr\f[R]
containers with their memory resource; the forms with \f[CR]target\f[R]
need a default constructor of \f[CR]T\f[R].
A struct with a default constructor is made with it, and then each
described member is set.
A class with no default constructor is made by a constructor that takes
the described members in the order of the description: decode reads them
into a \f[CR]std::tuple\f[R] and calls \f[CR]std::make_from_tuple\f[R].
An element of a container with an allocator makes that tuple with the
allocator of the container.
\f[CR]not_enough_memory\f[R].
.SS cbor::schema<T>::path
Opens a message and gives the root cbor::schema::accessor.
An rvalue moves into a new owner.
An owner is read in place.
Nothing is copied.
An lvalue \f[CR]std::string\f[R] does not compile.
\f[CR]path\f[R] checks the length, the table, the directory and the tag
of the root once.
It reads no field.
.SS cbor::schema<T>::at_path
Reads one value at \f[CR]Path\f[R] and returns a
\f[CR]std::expected\f[R] of it.
It makes the checks of cbor::schema::path() and of an accessor in one
call and keeps nothing.
It exists only for a value that holds no view into the bytes: a number,
a \f[CR]bool\f[R], an enum, or a \f[CR]std::optional\f[R] of one.
A path starts with \f[CR]$\f[R], names a member with \f[CR].name\f[R]
and an element with \f[CR][n]\f[R].
\f[CR][]\f[R] takes its index from the next argument.
On an array or a vector of \f[CR]std::uint8_t\f[R] or
\f[CR]std::byte\f[R], \f[CR][n]\f[R] reads one byte.
A text, a list or a struct needs an cbor::schema::accessor.
A member that \f[CR]T\f[R] does not have, a wrong count of indexes, or a
path to a text does not compile.
.SS cbor::schema<T>::unverified_path
Opens a message as cbor::schema::path() does, and does not check the
data of the message.
It checks every bound, so no read leaves the input.
Use it for a message from a peer that you trust.
\f[CR]unverified_path\f[R] gives an \f[CR]accessor<T, false>\f[R].
Every accessor that it gives on a path is unverified too, and so is each
element of a vector of fixed\-size structs.
\f[CR]view\f[R] of an unverified accessor does not check the tag of the
typed array.
A wrong byte of data gives a value, not an error.
.PP
Not checked:
.IP \(bu 2
the tag of each struct;
.IP \(bu 2
the head of a number, a \f[CR]bool\f[R], a fixed string and a
\f[CR]std::array\f[R];
.IP \(bu 2
the head byte of a text, a list and a map, and the tag of a typed array;
.IP \(bu 2
the prefix of a vector of fixed\-size structs;
.IP \(bu 2
the start of the table and the fillers before the items;
.IP \(bu 2
that the length of a typed array is a multiple of its element size;
.IP \(bu 2
that a \f[CR]bool\f[R] byte is 0 or 1: any other byte reads as
\f[CR]true\f[R];
.IP \(bu 2
in \f[CR]unverified_decode\f[R], the order of the references and of the
items.
.PP
Checked:
.IP \(bu 2
that each head, length and item lies inside the message;
.IP \(bu 2
that each directory entry lies between the start and the end of the
items;
.IP \(bu 2
each index against the count of its list, its table or its directory;
.IP \(bu 2
the limits of the build and, in \f[CR]unverified_decode\f[R], the
nesting depth.
.SS cbor::schema<T>::unverified_at_path
Reads one value as cbor::schema::at_path() does, and does not check the
data of the message.
The checks and the errors of cbor::schema::unverified_path() apply.
.SS cbor::schema<T>::unverified_decode
Reads the whole message as cbor::schema::decode() does, and does not
check the data of the message.
The checks and the errors of cbor::schema::unverified_path() apply; it
also checks the nesting depth.
It does not check the order of the references and of the items.
.SS cbor::schema<T>::description
\f[CR]description()\f[R] gives the table entries that are the same in
every message of \f[CR]T\f[R]: the 114 entry of each struct and the
description of each element type of a vector of fixed\-size structs
(\f[CR]wire\f[R]).
The bytes are a CBOR sequence (RFC 8742).
The compiler makes them.
Each of \f[CR]encode\f[R], \f[CR]path\f[R], \f[CR]at_path\f[R],
\f[CR]decode\f[R] and the unverified forms takes
\f[CR]description_place\f[R] as a template argument.
The default is \f[CR]inside\f[R]: the message holds the description.
With \f[CR]apart\f[R], \f[CR]encode\f[R] writes the message without the
description, and the read forms read such a message.
Use it to store the description once and the data of each key in another
place, for example in two named databases of LMDB.
The data form is one well\-formed CBOR item: tag 113 on the table
without the description and the rump.
The count of the table counts only the entries that it holds.
The references keep the indices of the message with the description, so
only \f[CR]cbor::schema<T>\f[R] with \f[CR]apart\f[R] reads the data
form.
A generic decoder of Packed CBOR does not.
The size of the data form plus the size of \f[CR]description()\f[R] is
the size of the message.
Every other error of cbor::schema::path(), cbor::schema::at_path() and
cbor::schema::decode().
.SS cbor::description_place
Tells if a message holds the description of its structs
(\f[CR]inside\f[R]) or not (\f[CR]apart\f[R]).
Each of \f[CR]encode\f[R], \f[CR]path\f[R], \f[CR]at_path\f[R],
\f[CR]decode\f[R] and the unverified forms takes it as a template
argument; the default is \f[CR]inside\f[R].
The data form of \f[CR]apart\f[R] and cbor::schema::description()
together have the size of the message.
.SS cbor::schema<T>::fixed_size
Gives the count of bytes that a field of type \f[CR]U\f[R] takes in a
message of \f[CR]T\f[R].
With no argument, it gives the size of the root record of \f[CR]T\f[R].
The root record is the end of the message.
A number takes its head and its argument of fixed width.
A struct takes its class tag, its table reference, a record head of 5
bytes and its fields.
A text, a list, a map, an optional of variable size or a
\f[CR]std::variant\f[R] takes a reference \f[CR]6(N)\f[R] of 6 bytes.
None.
A type that has no fixed size, for example a struct with a base class,
does not compile.
.SS cbor::schema<T>::member_offset
Gives the offset of the head of \f[CR]Member\f[R] from the start of the
record of \f[CR]U\f[R].
\f[CR]Member\f[R] is a pointer to a described member, \f[CR]&U::m\f[R].
With reflection, \f[CR]Member\f[R] can also be a reflection of the
member, \f[CR]\(ha\(haU::m\f[R].
The record starts with the class tag, the table reference and the record
head.
The fields follow the key order of RFC 8949 4.2.1: a shorter key first,
then the bytes of the key.
This order is not the order of declaration.
The root record of \f[CR]T\f[R] starts at
\f[CR]size \- fixed_size()\f[R] of the message.
None.
A \f[CR]Member\f[R] of another struct, or a member that the description
does not list, does not compile.
.SS cbor::schema::accessor<U, Verified>
Reads the part \f[CR]U\f[R] of a message of \f[CR]T\f[R].
A path that starts with \f[CR]\(at\f[R] starts at \f[CR]U\f[R].
A path that starts with \f[CR]$\f[R] starts at the root.
The result is a \f[CR]std::expected\f[R]: the value of a number,
\f[CR]bool\f[R] or enum; a \f[CR]std::string_view\f[R] of a text; the
accessor of a struct, a list or a map.
Every result holds the owner of the bytes.
The accessor of a \f[CR]std::variant<A...>\f[R] member has
\f[CR]index()\f[R] and \f[CR]get<A>()\f[R].
\f[CR]index()\f[R] gives the index of the alternative that the head of
its item names.
\f[CR]get<A>()\f[R] gives a class alternative as its accessor, a number,
\f[CR]bool\f[R] or enum as its value, and a text or byte string as a
\f[CR]std::string_view\f[R] into the message.
Another alternative gives \f[CR]incorrect_type\f[R].
A path ends at a variant member: \f[CR]$.pet.name\f[R] does not compile.
\f[CR]view\f[R] gives a cbor::typed_array_view of a list of numbers.
It exists on the root accessor only.
\f[CR]size()\f[R] gives the count of a list or a map.
An accessor of a vector of fixed\-size structs is a random access range
of \f[CR]fixed_element\f[R] accessors.
\f[CR]at_path\f[R] on a temporary does not compile.
.SS cbor::schema::accessor<fixed_element<S>, Verified>
Reads one element of type \f[CR]S\f[R] in a vector of fixed\-size
structs.
Such a vector is one byte string of its elements under tag 32873
(\f[CR]wire\f[R]).
A path that starts with \f[CR]\(at\f[R] reads a member of the element at
its offset.
A path that starts with \f[CR]$\f[R] starts at the root.
\f[CR]*it\f[R] of the cbor::schema::accessor of the vector, and
\f[CR]at_path<\(dq\(at[]\(dq>\f[R] on it, give this accessor.
It holds the owner of the bytes.
A path with \f[CR]$\f[R] gives the errors of cbor::schema::accessor.
\f[CR]at_path\f[R] on a temporary does not compile.
.SS cbor::typed_array_view<E>
A random access, sized range over an RFC 8746 typed array in the
message.
\f[CR]accessor::view\f[R] makes it.
It holds the owner of the bytes.
Each element is a value of \f[CR]E\f[R], read from little endian, not a
reference.
\f[CR]begin\f[R] and \f[CR]end\f[R] on a temporary do not compile.
.SS cbor::tag
The tag number of a struct, in the member \f[CR]tag\f[R] of its
cbor::describe_members specialization.
\f[CR]schema\f[R] writes the number as the first item of each record of
the struct.
A read compares it, so a message of another struct gives
\f[CR]incorrect_type\f[R].
The tag of a record of a \f[CR]std::variant\f[R] member chooses a class
alternative; a head of no alternative is \f[CR]incorrect_type\f[R].
Each alternative has its own number, also when it derives from the class
of another number.
\f[CR]structs\f[R] takes the same description.
These do not compile in \f[CR]schema<T>\f[R]: a struct in \f[CR]T\f[R]
with no description or with \f[CR]cbor::untagged\f[R]; two structs in
\f[CR]T\f[R] with one number, the alternatives of a variant included; a
number of a standard type (0, 1, 2, 3, 24, 64 to 87, 100, 258, 1004 and
32873); a number that the IANA registry calls always invalid (21334,
65535, 4294967295 and 18446744073709551615).
.SS The bytes that cbor::schema::encode writes
A message is Packed CBOR
(\f[CR]refs/draft\-ietf\-cbor\-packed\-19.txt\f[R]):
.IP
.EX
113([table, rump])
.EE
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l l.
T{
Part
T}@T{
Bytes
T}
_
T{
tag 113
T}@T{
Basic\-Packed\-CBOR, on an array of two
T}
T{
table
T}@T{
an array with a count of 4 bytes
T}
T{
114([keys])
T}@T{
the record function: the keys of one struct, one entry for each struct
T}
T{
description
T}@T{
\f[CR][class tag, [[key, RFC 8746 tag], ...]]\f[R]: one entry for each
element type of a vector of fixed\-size structs
T}
T{
directory
T}@T{
a byte string of 4\-byte offsets of the parts of variable size
T}
T{
fillers
T}@T{
\f[CR]undefined\f[R], so that the parts start at index 16 or later
T}
T{
parts
T}@T{
the texts, byte strings, lists, maps and the items of variant members
T}
T{
rump
T}@T{
the root record, at the end of the message
T}
.TE
.PP
A record is the class tag (tag), a straight reference tag 128 to 135 (or
\f[CR]6([n, ...])\f[R]) to its 114 entry, and an array with a count of 4
bytes of its fields.
Each field has a head of fixed width.
A field of variable size is \f[CR]6(N)\f[R], a shared reference with a
4\-byte \f[CR]N\f[R], into the table.
.PP
A \f[CR]std::variant\f[R] member is a field \f[CR]6(N)\f[R].
Its part is the item of the alternative that the variant holds: the
record of a class, the field of a number, \f[CR]bool\f[R] or enum with
its head of fixed width, or a text or byte string with a 4\-byte length.
The head of the item names the alternative; no two alternatives begin
with one head.
.PP
A list of a number is an RFC 8746 typed array, little endian.
A \f[CR]std::vector<double>\f[R] is tag 86.
.PP
A vector of fixed\-size structs is
\f[CR]32873([simple(j), h\(aq...\(aq])\f[R].
\f[CR]j\f[R] is the index of its description in the table; from index 16
on, \f[CR]6(N)\f[R] stands in place of \f[CR]simple(j)\f[R].
The byte string holds the elements with no padding, little endian.
Tag 32873 is provisional: the IANA registry does not have it yet.
.PP
A decoder of Packed CBOR reads the message as a map with the keys of the
members.
.SH ERRORS
.SS cbor::schema<T>::encode
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]no_buffer_space\f[R]: a \f[CR]std::span<char>\f[R] is shorter than
the message
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]nesting_depth_exceeded\f[R]: the value is deeper than the stack of
the thread holds
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]value_too_large\f[R]: the message is longer than 2\(ha32 \- 1
bytes
.SS cbor::schema<T>::decode
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]too_little_data\f[R]: the message is shorter than its table and
root
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]incorrect_type\f[R]: a message of another struct, or a wrong head;
a variant item whose head names no alternative
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]syntax_error\f[R], \f[CR]unpopulated_table_index\f[R]: a broken
directory
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]inadmissible_type_for_tag_content\f[R]: a typed array whose length
is not a multiple of its element size
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]nesting_depth_exceeded\f[R]: a variant item, like a list, a map
and an optional, is one level deeper than its parent
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]not_enough_memory\f[R]: an allocation of the result fails
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]std::logic_error\f[R]: a null or empty owner
.SS cbor::schema<T>::path
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]too_little_data\f[R]: the message is shorter than its table and
root, or its end is missing
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]incorrect_type\f[R]: a message of another layout, or a wrong tag
of the root
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]std::logic_error\f[R]: a null or empty owner
.SS cbor::schema<T>::at_path
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]too_little_data\f[R], \f[CR]incorrect_type\f[R]: as
cbor::schema::path()
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]index_out_of_bounds\f[R]: an index past the end of a list
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]unpopulated_table_index\f[R], \f[CR]syntax_error\f[R]: a broken
reference
.SS cbor::schema<T>::unverified_path
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]too_little_data\f[R]: a message that ends too early, or a length
past the end
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]index_out_of_bounds\f[R]: an index past the end of a list
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]unpopulated_table_index\f[R], \f[CR]syntax_error\f[R]: a reference
or a directory entry outside the table or the items
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]incorrect_type\f[R]: a variant item whose head names no
alternative.
The head chooses the alternative, so these forms compare it too
.SS cbor::schema<T>::description
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]incorrect_type\f[R]: a message with the description read with
\f[CR]apart\f[R], or the data form read with \f[CR]inside\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]too_little_data\f[R]: the data form read with \f[CR]inside\f[R],
when it is shorter than the least message of \f[CR]T\f[R]
.SS cbor::schema::accessor<U, Verified>
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]index_out_of_bounds\f[R]: an index past the end of a list
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]incorrect_type\f[R]: a wrong head or tag on the path; a variant
item whose head names no alternative; \f[CR]get<A>()\f[R] of another
alternative
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]unpopulated_table_index\f[R], \f[CR]syntax_error\f[R]: a broken
reference
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]std::logic_error\f[R]: a call on a moved\-from accessor
.SS cbor::schema::accessor<fixed_element<S>, Verified>
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]index_out_of_bounds\f[R]: an index past the end of the vector,
from the step that gave the element
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]std::logic_error\f[R]: a call on a moved\-from accessor
.SS cbor::typed_array_view<E>
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]at\f[R], \f[CR]first\f[R], \f[CR]last\f[R]:
\f[CR]index_out_of_bounds\f[R] as a value
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]std::logic_error\f[R]: \f[CR]operator[]\f[R] outside the view, or
\f[CR]front\f[R] and \f[CR]back\f[R] on an empty view
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS cbor::schema<T>::encode
.IP
.EX
\f[B]auto\f[R] const bytes = cbor::schema<tire>::encode(tire{17, 2.5f});
std::string out = \(dqx\(dq;
\f[B]auto\f[R] const n = cbor::schema<tire>::encode(tire{17, 2.5f}, out);  \f[I]// out.size() == 1 + *n\f[R]
.EE
.SS cbor::schema<T>::decode
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// bytes: a std::string from schema<car>::encode(car{\(dqVolvo\(dq, 5, ...})\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const back = cbor::schema<car>::decode(std::move(bytes));
std::string_view const make = back ? (*back)\->make : \(dq\(dq;  \f[I]// \(dqVolvo\(dq\f[R]
.EE
.SS cbor::schema<T>::path
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// bytes: a std::string from schema<car>::encode(car{\(dqVolvo\(dq, 5, ...})\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const root = cbor::schema<car>::path(std::move(bytes));
\f[B]auto\f[R] const make = root\->at_path<\(dq$.make\(dq>();  \f[I]// \(dqVolvo\(dq\f[R]
.EE
.SS cbor::schema<T>::at_path
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// bytes: from schema<car>::encode(car{\(dqVolvo\(dq, 5, {{17, 2.5f}, {18, 2.25f}}, {1.0, 2.0, 3.0}})\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const seats = cbor::schema<car>::at_path<\(dq$.seats\(dq>(bytes);                 \f[I]// 5\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const second = cbor::schema<car>::at_path<\(dq$.tires[1].diameter\(dq>(bytes);    \f[I]// 18\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const past = cbor::schema<car>::at_path<\(dq$.tires[].diameter\(dq>(bytes, 5uz); \f[I]// error::index_out_of_bounds\f[R]
.EE
.SS cbor::schema<T>::unverified_path
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// bytes: from schema<car>::encode(car{\(dqVolvo\(dq, 5, ...}), a peer that the program trusts wrote them\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const seats = cbor::schema<car>::unverified_at_path<\(dq$.seats\(dq>(bytes);  \f[I]// 5\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const lane = cbor::schema<car>::unverified_path(std::string(bytes));
\f[B]auto\f[R] const diameter = lane\->at_path<\(dq$.tires[1].diameter\(dq>();               \f[I]// 18\f[R]
.EE
.SS cbor::schema<T>::unverified_at_path
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// bytes: from schema<car>::encode(car{\(dqVolvo\(dq, 5, ...}), a peer that the program trusts wrote them\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const seats = cbor::schema<car>::unverified_at_path<\(dq$.seats\(dq>(bytes);  \f[I]// 5\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const lane = cbor::schema<car>::unverified_path(std::string(bytes));
\f[B]auto\f[R] const diameter = lane\->at_path<\(dq$.tires[1].diameter\(dq>();               \f[I]// 18\f[R]
.EE
.SS cbor::schema<T>::unverified_decode
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// bytes: from schema<car>::encode(car{\(dqVolvo\(dq, 5, ...}), a peer that the program trusts wrote them\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const seats = cbor::schema<car>::unverified_at_path<\(dq$.seats\(dq>(bytes);  \f[I]// 5\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const lane = cbor::schema<car>::unverified_path(std::string(bytes));
\f[B]auto\f[R] const diameter = lane\->at_path<\(dq$.tires[1].diameter\(dq>();               \f[I]// 18\f[R]
.EE
.SS cbor::schema<T>::description
.IP
.EX
\f[B]constexpr\f[R] \f[B]auto\f[R] apart = cbor::description_place::apart;
\f[B]auto\f[R] const data = cbor::schema<car>::encode<apart>(car{\(dqVolvo\(dq, 5, {{17, 2.5f}, {18, 2.25f}}, {1.0, 2.0, 3.0}});
\f[B]auto\f[R] const description = cbor::schema<car>::description();
std::size_t const size = data\->size() + description.size();          \f[I]// the size of the message\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const seats = cbor::schema<car>::at_path<\(dq$.seats\(dq, apart>(*data);  \f[I]// 5\f[R]
.EE
.SS cbor::description_place
.IP
.EX
\f[B]constexpr\f[R] \f[B]auto\f[R] apart = cbor::description_place::apart;
\f[B]auto\f[R] const data = cbor::schema<car>::encode<apart>(car{\(dqVolvo\(dq, 5, {{17, 2.5f}, {18, 2.25f}}, {1.0, 2.0, 3.0}});
\f[B]auto\f[R] const description = cbor::schema<car>::description();
std::size_t const size = data\->size() + description.size();          \f[I]// the size of the message\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const seats = cbor::schema<car>::at_path<\(dq$.seats\(dq, apart>(*data);  \f[I]// 5\f[R]
.EE
.SS cbor::schema<T>::fixed_size
.IP
.EX
\f[B]constexpr\f[R] std::size_t root_record = cbor::schema<tire>::fixed_size();         \f[I]// 18\f[R]
\f[B]constexpr\f[R] std::size_t number = cbor::schema<tire>::fixed_size<std::uint16_t>(); \f[I]// 3\f[R]
\f[B]constexpr\f[R] std::size_t text = cbor::schema<car>::fixed_size<std::string>();     \f[I]// 6\f[R]
.EE
.SS cbor::schema<T>::member_offset
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// tire: std::uint16_t diameter; float pressure;\f[R]
\f[B]constexpr\f[R] std::size_t diameter = cbor::schema<tire>::member_offset<&tire::diameter>();  \f[I]// 10\f[R]
\f[B]constexpr\f[R] std::size_t pressure = cbor::schema<tire>::member_offset<&tire::pressure>();  \f[I]// 13\f[R]
\f[I]// bytes[bytes.size() \- fixed_size() + 13] is 0xfa, the head of a float\f[R]
.EE
.SS cbor::schema::accessor<U, Verified>
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// bytes: from schema<car>::encode(car{\(dqVolvo\(dq, 5, {{17, 2.5f}, {18, 2.25f}}, {1.0, 2.0, 3.0}})\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const root = cbor::schema<car>::path(std::move(bytes));
\f[B]auto\f[R] const tires = root\->at_path<\(dq$.tires\(dq>();
\f[B]auto\f[R] const count = tires\->size();                       \f[I]// 2\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const second = tires\->at_path<\(dq\(at[1].diameter\(dq>();  \f[I]// 18\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const seats = tires\->at_path<\(dq$.seats\(dq>();         \f[I]// 5\f[R]
\f[B]for\f[R] (\f[B]auto\f[R] const t : *tires)
    seen.push_back(*t.at_path<\(dq\(at.diameter\(dq>());         \f[I]// 17, then 18\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const track = root\->view<\(dq$.track\(dq>();             \f[I]// 3 numbers\f[R]
.EE
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// home: struct { std::variant<dog, cat> pet; }; bytes: from schema<home>::encode(home{dog{{\(dqrex\(dq}, 3}})\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const doc = cbor::schema<home>::path(std::string(*bytes));
\f[B]auto\f[R] const pet = doc\->at_path<\(dq$.pet\(dq>();
\f[B]auto\f[R] const held = pet\->index();                         \f[I]// 0\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const name = pet\->get<dog>()\->at_path<\(dq\(at.name\(dq>();  \f[I]// \(dqrex\(dq\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const other = pet\->get<cat>();                     \f[I]// error::incorrect_type\f[R]
.EE
.SS cbor::schema::accessor<fixed_element<S>, Verified>
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// bytes: from schema<car>::encode(car{\(dqVolvo\(dq, 5, {{17, 2.5f}, {18, 2.25f}}, {1.0, 2.0, 3.0}})\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const root = cbor::schema<car>::path(std::move(bytes));
\f[B]auto\f[R] const second = root\->at_path<\(dq$.tires[]\(dq>(1uz);
\f[B]auto\f[R] const diameter = second\->at_path<\(dq\(at.diameter\(dq>();  \f[I]// 18\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const seats = second\->at_path<\(dq$.seats\(dq>();        \f[I]// 5\f[R]
.EE
.SS cbor::typed_array_view<E>
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// bytes: from schema<car>::encode(car{..., {1.0, 2.0, 3.0}}), the member track\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const root = cbor::schema<car>::path(std::move(bytes));
\f[B]auto\f[R] const track = root\->view<\(dq$.track\(dq>();
double const sum = std::accumulate(track\->begin(), track\->end(), 0.0);  \f[I]// 6\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const past = track\->at(3);                                        \f[I]// error::index_out_of_bounds\f[R]
.EE
.SS cbor::tag
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// point: a struct whose description has the tag 1802\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const bytes = cbor::schema<point>::encode(point{1, 2});
std::size_t const root = bytes\->size() \- cbor::schema<point>::fixed_size();  \f[I]// the root record starts with d9 07 0a\f[R]
.EE
